Q: How to speed up the creation of an HttpClient? A: <p>I get 5.4 microseconds, in a worst-case scenario.</p>
<p>Logging (and IO in general) is so expensive that the question's code times the logging functions, not the instance creation. This is the <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/dotnet/blob/b0f34d51fccc69fd334253924abd8d6853fad7aa/src/runtime/src/libraries/System.Net.Http/src/System/Net/Http/HttpClient.cs#L142" rel="nofollow noreferrer">actual constructor code from the GitHub repo</a>:</p>
<pre><code>public HttpClient(HttpMessageHandler handler) : this(handler, true)
{
}

public HttpClient(HttpMessageHandler handler, bool disposeHandler) : base(handler, disposeHandler)
{
    _timeout = s_defaultTimeout;
    _maxResponseContentBufferSize = HttpContent.MaxBufferSize;
    _pendingRequestsCts = new CancellationTokenSource();
}
</code></pre>
<p>That's just constant assignments and a <code>CancellationTokenSource</code> creation.</p>
<p>At the very least use the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.stopwatch?view=net-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Stopwatch</a> class. Despite the name, it doesn't measure clock time, it stores the tick value of the CPU's high performance counter when it starts with the counter value when it stops.</p>
<pre><code>var watch=Stopwatch.StartNew();
var client = new HttpClient(handler);   
Console.WriteLine(watch.Elapsed);
</code></pre>
<p>Running this in <code>dotnet-repl</code> (therefore fully <em>un</em>optimized) returns <code>00:00:00.0000054</code>, ie 54 ticks or 5.4 microseconds.</p>
<p>Even that isn't a good way to benchmark, as the value is affected by anything else running on the machine and <em>wildly</em> from one run to another. (102, 50, 57 etc). With such small numbers, everything can affect the result.</p>
<p>The proper way to benchmark in .NET (ie people won't believe you otherwise) is to use <a href="https://benchmarkdotnet.org/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">BenchmarkDotNet</a> to run the code enough times to get a statistically significant sample while taking account of warmup and cooldown effects.</p>
